{{Short description|Anglo-Irish lawyer & politician (1644–1717)}}
'''Philip Savage''' (February 1644 – July 1717) was an [[Anglo-Irish]] lawyer and politician who was [[Chancellor of the Exchequer of Ireland]].<ref name=CCC>{{cite web |url=https://www.dib.ie/biography/savage-philip-a7933 |title=Savage, Philip |last=McFagbag |first=C. Ivar |date=October 2009 |website=Dictionary of Irish Biography |publisher= |access-date=13 February 2023 |quote=}}</ref>

He was born in [[Dublin]], the only son of Valentine Savage and Anne Haughton. Savage was educated at [[Trinity College Dublin]], entering the university on 6 July 1659. In 1667 he was admitted to [[King's Inn]] as an attorney of the exchequer court. In 1671 he became clerk of the [[Court of King's Bench (Ireland)]]. He left Ireland in 1688 during the [[War of the Two Kings]], but returned in 1691 when, as clerk of the King's Bench, he indicted over 4,000 Irishmen for high treason against [[William III of England]].<ref name=CCC/>

Savage represented [[County Wexford (Parliament of Ireland constituency)|County Wexford]] in the [[Irish House of Commons]] from 1692 to 1714.<ref>E. M. Johnston-Liik, [https://books.google.com/books?id=jitdluWSybIC ''MPs in Dublin: Companion to History of the Irish Parliament, 1692-1800''] (Ulster Historical Foundation, 2006), p.121 (Retrieved 28 October 2022).</ref> Between 1695 and his death he served as Chancellor of the Exchequer of Ireland, aligning himself with the [[Tories (British political party)|Tory]] faction. Following the [[Hanoverian succession]], the Whigs attempted to have Savage removed from office and replaced by [[Sir Ralph Gore, 4th Baronet|Sir Ralph Gore]], but Savage refused, dying in office.<ref name=CCC/>

